directAnswer:
Is the belief about the CSS right? Partly. It is wrong about size and right about the primitives.

Wrong about size (measured two ways). The 475 KB sheet costs about 5 ms to parse for each document. A sheet with only the 766 rules that the page can use (189 KB) gives the same first style and layout time: 111 ms against 110 ms. A sheet two times as large adds 4 ms of parse and 13 ms. So you do not generate too much CSS for speed, and the lab does nothing unnecessary there. The lab even avoids a problem of the real app: in dev the lab sends one 518 KB sheet, and the real app sends 1.6 MB with 4 copies on the run route (audit SHELL-05).

Right about the primitives, for a different reason. In a production build, 67% of the main-thread work of an ArrowDown is style and layout (92 of 138 ms), and almost all of it comes from two things in the primitive CSS that work together:

1. The nav glider in the screenshot list. Its rules make the Nav root a `:has()` anchor.
2. A rule shape that makes Chrome invalidate the whole subtree of each `:has()` anchor. One rule of this shape anywhere in the sheet is enough, also when no element matches it:

```css
/* slow: a :has() compound, then a combinator, then * (also inside :is() or :not()) */
.zz-never:has(.qq-never) * { --zz: 1 }
.zz-never:has(.qq-never) > * { --zz: 1 }
.yy-never:is(.zz-never:has(.qq-never) *) { --zz: 1 }
/* fast */
.zz-never:has(.qq-never) .yy-never { --zz: 1 }
.zz-never:has(.qq-never) { --zz: 1 }
```

With one such rule, each change of the selected row recalculates 1,233 elements. Without it, 167. Chrome names it in its own trace: "Invalidation set invalidates subtree" on `div.nav`, `allDescendantsMightBeInvalid: true`, change `pseudo=has`.

Where the time goes when you use the lab, largest first:

1. In the gallery and the compare view: the live frames (lab only). Each frame is a whole app. One frame in dev is 417 requests and 20.4 MB (lucide-react alone is one file of 5.7 MB). The gallery is 15 frames: about 4,890 requests in dev and 6 to 8 s of main-thread work. A production build still needs about 5 s, so this is not only development mode.
2. Inside one frame in dev: React development mode with StrictMode (tool stack, same in the real app). Script is 3.8 to 5.7 times the production script. StrictMode comes from the default client entry of TanStack Start and renders each component two times. Without it, ArrowDown goes from 141 to 115 ms and A from 221 to 138 ms.
3. In any build: the glider and the rule shape above (primitives, same in the real app). ArrowDown 88 ms goes to 37 ms without the list glider. The list scroll goes from 848 ms to 57 ms for 10 wheel steps.
4. With all folders open (585 rows): about 400 ms for ArrowDown, also after both fixes. The trace shows a `:has()` invalidation of the whole document from `body` (2,432 elements). I did not find the rule.

So: the lab is slow mostly because it shows many live frames from a development server. That part will not come to the real app. The primitives add a cost that will come with the new design, and it has a clear fix that I measured.

